diff --git a/docs/DUPLICATE.md b/docs/DUPLICATE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a8b826c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/DUPLICATE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,99 @@
+# Duplicate evaluation
+
+`GameRule.duplicate` enables an experimental four-player riichi evaluation
+format. It defaults to `False` in both platform presets. Ordinary games
+retain their existing wall and match rules. Three-player duplicate games
+are rejected.
+
+```python
+import riichienv
+
+rule = riichienv.GameRule.default_tenhou()
+rule.duplicate = True
+for candidate_seat in range(4):
+    env = riichienv.RiichiEnv(game_mode=2, seed=417, rule=rule)
+    observations = env.reset(oya=0)
+    # Run the candidate at candidate_seat, references at the other seats.
+    # Reset each policy's memory between matches.
+```
+
+The engine fixes the board for each seat; the evaluator rotates policies,
+not the wall or physical seat identities. Compare complete groups of four
+matches and treat each seed group as a single statistical cluster.
+
+## Draw contract
+
+After the ordinary deal of 13 tiles to each seat, the 70 live-wall tiles
+are assigned in ordinary draw order, round-robin from the dealer. This gives
+18 tiles each to the dealer and the next seat, and 17 to each remaining seat.
+The dealer's opening draw is the first tile in its queue. Across all four
+dealer positions each physical seat receives the same total allocation.
+
+Every subsequent draw consumes the next physical tile of the drawing seat's
+queue. Chi and pon may skip turns but cannot reassign tiles to another seat.
+Ankan, daiminkan, and kakan also draw from the caller's own queue. Kan can
+therefore advance that player's sequence, but cannot change anyone else's
+sequence. This fixes draw prefixes by seat, not the number of draws before
+a win, the timing of those draws, or the results of calls.
+
+Kan replacements retain rinshan scoring, chankan, kan limits, and the usual
+kan-dora reveal timing. The original 14 dead-wall tiles stay reserved;
+initial/kan dora and ura indicators use their original positions. Replacement
+tiles do not come from the ordinary four rinshan slots. All physical tiles
+remain unique; no stream is replenished by sampling with replacement.
+
+The first player to consume its final queued tile makes the last draw of
+the hand. A normal final draw permits haitei; a kan replacement remains
+rinshan. After its discard, ron remains possible but chi/pon/kan do not.
+If nobody wins, the engine applies normal exhaustive-draw settlement.
+Some other seats' queued tiles consequently remain unused.
+
+## Match and seed contract
+
+Single-hand games finish after one hand. East games advance through East 4;
+South games advance through South 4. Every completed or aborted hand advances
+the dealer. A dealer win or dealer-tenpai exhaustive draw pays 400 from every
+other player to the dealer, in place of a dealer repeat. There are no dealer
+repeats, early bankruptcy endings, agari-yame, or extra West/South rounds.
+Honba follows the ordinary win/draw progression even as the dealer advances;
+scores and unclaimed riichi deposits carry forward, with normal final scoring.
+A game reset at a later round/dealer plays the remaining scheduled hands.
+
+For generated walls, the episode seed is first mixed with SplitMix64. The
+scheduled hand position (`4 * round_wind + dealer`) is then added and mixed
+again with the existing shuffle seed mixer. Adjacent episode seeds therefore
+do not systematically reuse one another's hands. Wins, calls, draws, aborts, and earlier hand lengths cannot perturb a
+later board. Resetting with the same seed, round, and dealer reproduces it;
+cloning also preserves partially consumed queues. `reset(wall=...)` accepts
+a complete custom physical wall using the same dealing/draw convention.
+
+The `start_game` replay event marks duplicate games with `duplicate: true`.
+Duplicate `tsumo` events also carry the public `tiles_left` count, including
+zero on the final personal draw. Duplicate observations expose this count
+as `tiles_left`, and their native feature encoders use it. Ordinary observations
+leave this optional override unset and retain their existing encoding.
+Replay-based feature producers must honor the public count instead of always
+subtracting draws from 70. Other MJAI hand/action/result events are preserved.
+Observation legal actions come from the duplicate engine; policy inputs do
+not receive future queues. Keep these results separate from standard-riichi match results:
+the altered wall and match rules can change which policies perform best.
+
+This is a riichi-specific experimental variant inspired by
+[Mahjong International League duplicate rules](https://mahjong-mil.org/rules_dup/),
+not an implementation of its MCR scoring or IMP system.
+
+## Local build
+
+This checkout is based on upstream `v0.4.10`; its Python wheel identifies itself
+as `0.4.10+duplicate`. Build and install from the source checkout:
+
+```bash
+maturin build --release --interpreter /path/to/python
+/path/to/python -m pip install --no-deps --force-reinstall target/wheels/*.whl
+```
+
+All hand-solving and scoring code matches upstream; the local changes are
+limited to the duplicate draw queues, scheduled rounds and their metadata.
+
+The duplicate integration scenarios live in `tests/env/test_duplicate.py`.
+The ordinary test suite verifies that the default rules still behave as before.
